Quick concise summary (3–4 sentences)
- The Pharaon returns to Marseilles; its young mate Armando Pascal takes command after Captain Leclere dies, and he plans to marry Abrielle, a Catalan girl. Rivalries and spite (especially from the supercargo Blaire and the tailor Corbin, plus the jealous Quinton) lead to an anonymous denunciation accusing Pascal of Bonapartist correspondence; he’s arrested through the actions of the ambitious deputy procureur Trentony de Shayne. In the Chateau d’If Pascal meets the erudite, imprisoned abbe (the “Trenton Davion”/Spada scholar), who discovers (and then dies protecting) a family will pointing to a vast hidden treasure on the island Brayan Annabel. Pascal later escapes a staged “burial at sea,” survives, is picked up by smugglers, and heads toward the island to recover the treasure while France is convulsed by Napoleon’s return (the Hundred Days) and its aftermath.

Key characters (short)
- Armando Pascal — young mate/maritime protagonist; brave, loyal, later imprisoned.
- Abrielle — beautiful Catalan girl; Pascal’s betrothed.
- M. Julianne — shipowner; Pascal’s patron.
- Blaire — supercargo; resentful antagonist.
- Corbin — Leghorn tailor; vulgar drunk, useful to conspirators.
- Quinton — Catalan cousin; jealous rival for Abrielle.
- Trenton Davion (the “abbé”) — learned Italian/secret-keeper; discovers Spada will; dies in prison.
- Trentony de Shayne — deputy procureur; ambitious, pivotal in Pascal’s arrest and later politicized maneuvers.
- M. Abe (Abe de Shayne) — Shayne’s father; political survivor with shady influence.
- Beatrix/the Genoese crew — sailors who rescue Pascal after his burial-at-sea and carry him toward Brayan Annabel.

Major themes & notes
- Injustice & false accusation: a single anonymous note destroys lives; legal power is fragile and politicized.
- Fate, patience, and perseverance: long imprisonment, slow plans (digging, study), and plotted escapes.
- Knowledge vs. power: the abbe’s scholarship and ciphered will contrast with the raw ambition of men like Shayne and Blaire.
- Betrayal, jealousy, and class rivalries: personal jealousies (Quinton, Blaire) intersect with political conspiracies.
- Historical backdrop: clearly set in 1815–16 around Marseilles, Elba, Napoleon’s return (Hundred Days) — politics directly shape individual fates.

